A||||13||V8Fiero||V8 water pump question..||||5||
Z||000000||V8Fiero||04-22-2001||11:17 PM||||This one is for all you guys with V8's out there. <P>I have the basic water pump set up on my V8 (short chevy mounted upside down)I also have a 4 core radiator.<P>Heres my question. Anyone know how many gpm the stock water pump flows? I have been thinking of upgrading to an electric one or just keep my stock one. <P>Of the electric pumps what would a person look for before purchasing one?<P>Any and all suggestions are appreciated.<BR>Im still not sure that my setup is going to cool it enough for my tastes..<P>Thanks guys/gals.<BR>Scott<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000001||Tina||04-22-2001||11:50 PM||||Hmmm, looking at electric ones.<BR>Cost $300 and a aprox. 2000 hour seal-life expectancy.<BR>The big advandange apperently is, since it doesn't run off your crank you get a couple more horsies to play with. <BR>Bad news is, according to my calculations in about 3 month I would need a new one for my"Daily Driver/Grocery getter" (Seal-life)<P>So I guess if you drive yours only occasionly, go for it.<BR>But if your the one stuck doing the errands,...well..... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>Tina<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Will||04-23-2001||11:06 AM||||Electric water pumps do <I>NOT</I> free up horsepower. They just shift the load from your water pump to your alternator.<P>Racers get an extra few horses from an electric w/p because they run it from a battery, not from an alternator (most don't have alternators).<P>What's wrong with you belt driven pump? It works, doesn't it?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Butter||04-23-2001||11:34 AM||||Been Running a CSI Electric Pump for about 2 years almost everyday to work trouble free.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Jimmy||04-23-2001||01:05 PM||||Paid $240 for my CSI waterpump.  Its one of the best.  CSI has tested them for over 5500 hours starit.  My buddy has used one on his Hotrod for over 3 years with no problems.  Theres a guy I talk to at the "Back to The Fifties" car show that has one on his big block that he uses to pull his trailer of cars around from show to show. He has been using it for over two years.   Easy to maintain if needed a full over haul (includes a new motor) is a five minute swap and costs $95.  And it does free up SOME HP, yes some of the load is transfered to the alternator, but that load is a smaller amount on the engine(5amps peak).  <P>This pump is better than the Moroso electric pump as the CSI flows 30-37 gph to the Moroso 19 gph.<P>BTW I see nothing wrong with Archies setup.  I just always wanted to use the CSI pump.  I can always go back to Archies setup in the future.<P>Jim  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Archie||04-23-2001||01:12 PM||||I've always felt that the CSI is a good alternative. I've used it on several customer cars when the customer had concerns about my regular setup.<P>In an effort to do a test this Summer on my "T" top car, I put one on it. The only problem keeping me from testing it on that car is that the stupid 4-puck clutch (that I'm was also going to be testing on that car) started slipping badly with less than 10 miles on it.<P>Now, I gotta tear that whole thing apart to R&R the clutch.<P>Archie||||reg||1
Z||000006||Jimmy||04-23-2001||01:20 PM||||Tina not trying to be a jerk, as I hate people pointing out my miscalculations, but are you saying you drive your car 22 hours a day!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>2000hours/3months or 93 days=21.5 hours per day.<P>I figure I will drive my car at the most 2 hours a day(I think that is way high as I don't drive it in the winter).  That will figure out to roughly under 3yrs.  Figure just summers and you have at least 5 years.  Thats fine by me as a full rebuild is $95 bucks.  <P>The only real downfall I see to the electric waterpump is that GM engineneers designed the engine with a waterpump that increases with speed/flow as the engine RPM increases.  The electric is always a constant.  Dunno know if this is good or bad, all I know is that belt driven has obviously been proven to work well!<P>You guys can probably see that I have put a lot of thought into this "What Waterpump for the V8" idea.  I'll try and post pics soon as my V8 swap is finishing up this week.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Jim||||reg||1
Z||000007||pherder||04-23-2001||04:40 PM||||FYI, I have one of the Aerospace Components Remote Mount Electric Water Pumps rated @ 37 GPM and using 4 amps to drive it ($240 in latest Jegs ... $250 for the SBC version).<P>In the same catalog, CSI Electric Water Pump (for the SBC) is rated @ 30 GPM, uses 5.8 amps to drive it and costs $260.<P>Which is better?  By the numbers, the Aerospace Components is <B>BUT</B> the cost to rebuild it and it's rated lifetime isn't stated so .... ?<P><B>IF</B> I really do the V8 swap (and it doesn't look good <B>again</B> since I was just laid off from work   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> ), I plan on mounting the remote water pump up front near the outlet of the radiator.<P><B>OR</B> I may have to do the same thing I did the <B>LAST</B> time I was almost ready to install my V8 ... sell it all off. <P>Paul<BR><p>[This message has been edited by pherder (edited 04-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||ray b||04-23-2001||05:45 PM||||why not use a boat style pump in acc location like in place of a/c or power steer<BR>pump on v-8.belt drive but next to motor not sticking out like stock unit?||||reg||1
Z||000009||Tina||04-23-2001||06:56 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Jimmy:<BR><B>2000hours/3months or 93 days=21.5 hours per day</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>O.K., got me.<BR>Slight miscalculation   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>But like you said,<BR>"all I know is that belt driven has obviously been proven to work well!"<BR>So why do you want to switch?<BR>I don't know about you,but I can come with a few ideas where I rather spend the extra $'s.<P>Tina  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||V8Fiero||04-23-2001||07:03 PM||||Tina,<P>I havent had my car on the road yet to really test the system with my new radiator. I have had the car idling in the driveway for a bit to see if it would overheat. I did notice the temp rising with it at idle and the fan off. With the fan on it would just take a little longer to reach the higher temp. Once I revved the engine up a little the temp would drop. Maybe just a little better of a water pump (stock style) would help....<P>Maybe Im being anal about this part but I think it is the most crucial.....  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>Thanks for all the input guys/gals <P>Scott||||reg||1
Z||000011||BBBAD GT||04-23-2001||09:45 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ray b:<BR><B>why not use a boat style pump in acc location like in place of a/c or power steer<BR>pump on v-8.belt drive but next to motor not sticking out like stock unit?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I actually saw a guy locally do this to his V8 conversion, sort of.  He used a 4 cyl water pump mounted on the side of the motor, like an acc, and he didn't have to cut anything (his Fiero interior looks like a boat, its yellow/white and all).  I was saying the same thing, how could a 4 cyl wp cool a big 350? He claims it stays at 190 all the time, I have a hard time believing it.....my archie setup works just fine.<BR><P>------------------<BR>        <IMG SRC="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero/V8sig.jpg">        <P><A HREF="http://home.att.net/~nomercy5.0/V8Specs.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD V8 For Sale</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD 5.0</A>||||reg||1
Z||000012||V8Fiero||04-23-2001||11:22 PM||||Do any of you have any special kind of short chevy pump? I see summit, jegs and the like have ones that say they are more efficent. When I bought mine I just went down to the local Champion auto store and got one for a 69 Chevelle? I think. Anyway it was only like 25 or 30 bucks.(Reman one) Could this be why mine isnt quite cooling like it should? I did re-purge the system this evening. Results arent in yet........<P>Thanks gang<BR>Scott||||reg||1
Z||000013||cardealer||04-24-2001||01:10 PM||||i am in almost the same situation, but i had an electric pump although it worked.. i was starting to have concearns about its effectiveness in very hot weather going down the highway. i went to my local hotrod shop to buy a edelbock waterpump, the owner sold me a "stewart" waterpump  (www.stewartcomponents.com ). he sais it flows better than the edelbrock. so cut hole in the fenderwell and set up a cog belt system. i dont know how well it is going to work, having starter problems. i hope to have it cured this weekend!<BR>good luck!||||reg||1
